<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Zhenzong of Song</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/968">968</a>–1022</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Zhao Heng</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Zhenzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> was the third emperor of the Northern Song dynasty, reigning from 997 to 1022. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> invasion of 1004 he concluded the Treaty of Chanyuan (1005), which bought lasting peace with annual payments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>. His reign saw the introduction of quick-ripening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/champa-rice">Champa rice</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> into southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and lavish state rituals such as the feng and shan sacrifices at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-tai">Mount Tai</a> in 1008.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Agriculture was transformed by the introduction under Emperor <b>Zhenzong</b> of early-ripening, drought-tolerant Champa rice from central Vietnam, which allowed double cropping in southern China and supported the period's rapid population growth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
